{
  "dom_id": "step-e7394",
  "name": "Example: Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution",
  "problem": "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ college professors\n\n- The number of questions answered correctly by a student in a $12$-question exam\n\n- The heights of $10$ elementary school children",
  "worked_solution": "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of questions answered in a $12$- question exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $12$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The heights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
